now you may think party conference  season is over but this week the Green  Party a gathering in Newport for their  annual get-together and the party's  co-leader Sean Barie joins us now from  there thanks very much for being on the  program this morning now the Green Party  believes there is a climate emergency  and if that is true then you're going to  have to take some pretty radical policy  policy decisions to tackle it what  exactly do you think needs to happen  well we need to change essentially every  single way that the economy works there  Caroline Lucas our conference yesterday  put forward some motion for a  sustainable economy act and that would  look at absolutely everything that we do  in the economy and switch that away from  carbon emissions from fossil fuels make  sure that we're building the sustainable  local economy that we need in the UK and  making sure for example that we deal  with the the city of London as Mayor of  London Candida I can say that we need to  engage with the with the city to make  sure that we're not using our financial  institutions to fund exploitation and  resource extraction in the global South  which also exposes people these are  really really big issues that we are  looking at in the round as the Green  Party at home for example cancelling hs2  committing that money to local transport  to making buses free across the country  all these are things that absolutely  need to change and need looking at in a  radical way in the road right through  the economy with this new act with  greens in the Treasury making those  decisions talking about radical ideas to  tackle climate change do we all need to  stop eating so much meat I think most  people recognize the impact that  particularly beef and lamb farming have  on carbon emissions and a lot of us is  looking to ways to cut down on the meat  in our diets and I think that's that's a  big thing that a lot of people are doing  and needs to be facilitated we are  seeing more products coming forwards  where you've got proteins that are that  are not meat but also your people think  doing things in work places like  meat-free Mondays those are really  really good initiatives and just cutting  shifting our agriculture away from using  our land third for me using it more for  plant-based diets and for the kind of  rewilding and reforestation that will  help with climate change as well those  are really important things that that  we've been talking about at this  conference and obviously Greens have  been talking about for 30 plus years as  well I mean how far would you go would  you consider a meat tax for example to  try and encourage this our taxation  system does need to change but I think  in terms of agriculture there the main  thing is working with farmers to see how  they can change their practices make  sure that any meat they produce is very  high welfare cut down on the overall  volume and really think about how we use  our land I think that's that's the most  important thing that we serve it's the  taxation force with the detail you are  looking after the land if the taxation  system needs to change would you  actually consider and meet tax then well  I think I mean we need to have a carbon  tax throughout the system and I think  possibly the right way to do it is not  to tax meat at the counter but also to  tax the the things that lead to the the  carbon emissions in the first place at  other points in one thing we've talked  about is having a tax on aviation fuel  it's absolutely ridiculous that aviation  fuel doesn't have a tax on it that's a  really big contributor to our carbon  emissions as well and that's something  that absolutely needs correcting at an  international levels those are things  that they absolutely need to be done now  we could be hurtling towards a general  election sooner rather than later what  discussions are the Green Party having  about potentially standing aside in  certain seats to allow other parties a  better shot at winning we're coming off  some of our best elections ever this  year we've more than doubled our number  of councilors with more than doubled our  number of em EPS in Europe and we're in  a very very strong position at the  moment and other parties are keen to  talk to us about working together and I  think it is important that all the  parties look at where we target our  resources because what we don't want to  see is a Tory  No Deal perhaps it push through by Tory  majority government and that's a real  risk we've run in the larger election  going forward so we are prepared to talk  to any party that wants to talk to us  about making fair arrangements for  fighting the general election in ways  that target our resources most  effectively  and obviously in the European elections  there are many seats so we came first or  second where we're strong challengers to  be MPs and it's very important that we  gain more MPs more green MPs like  Caroline Lucas in Parliament who can  make the case for these big changes that  we need to make on the ones in assembly  where I've been for three years we've  made big changes to Sadiq Khan's  policies we've pushed him in the right  direction  and greens are showing right around the  country and councils as well that way  you've got green voices in the room  things move in the right direction  things change people listen and people  are casting their votes with that in  mind as well so I think we're looking  forward to the general action and we are  talking to other parties about how we  can fight that most effectively in a way  to prevent the the crash out brexit that  the Conservatives would like to impose  on us  you mentioned Carolyn Lucas there of  course we know that the Green Party  holds one seat right in Pavillion are  there any other areas where you would  expect other parties to stand aside to  allow a Green Party candidate to have a  better shot well you'll understand why I  wouldn't talk about individual seats or  anything online in detail right now  because it's really important that we we  build trust with other parties as well  but certainly we are challenging you  know without any help from anybody else  for the seats in Sheffield and Bristol  and Norwich where we've done very well  in the past as well and we are the best  challengers to the Conservative Party on  the Isle of Wight as well so those seats  we can fight without anybody else's help  but I think you know other seats we are  we have you'll forgive me for not  talking about now you are of course in  the Green Party candidate for London  Maire what went through your mind when  you heard that Rory Stewart was going to  be entering the race I think what he  doesn't have is the records that the  Greens have in London we've had Green  Assembly members for 20 years making  every single Mayor of London do things  better making them listen to us and and  he needs to be making sure that he  understands London I mean I said that he  wants to walk through every borough as  if that's something special every single  mayoral candidate has to do that and  I've super mayor twice before this is  the third time I'll be standing last  time I was  third and I think potentially with the  Conservative Party votes split I've got  a very good chance of coming second in  first place votes and obviously the way  the election system works in London all  bets are off there as to who can come  first and I think Sadiq Khan needs the  strongest possible challenge he is  failing on a number of climate related  issues on clean air on housing he's not  pushing hard enough for the government  to give us more powers and and I'm a  very very good candidate to be putting  forward the case for all of those things  to be done and that's what London is  really want and what they really want  from their mayoral candidates two very  quick final thought what's your  favourite London pub my favourite lens  info I probably have to go for my local  which is the junction Tavern which is a  lot of my custom over the years okay  thank you very much Sean Barie thank you
 

